<Article slug="replication-and-failover">
<p>A product’s price changes from 12 to 15. The application commits that change to its database, then deletes the old Redis entry. Redis replies that it deleted one key. The next cache lookup should miss and load 15.</p>
<p>Now the Redis primary fails. A replica takes its place—and returns 12.</p>
<p>The database did not lose the price update. The application did not forget its invalidation. The deletion happened on a Redis process whose latest state did not reach the surviving copy. Follow that sequence below, first without delivering the deletion, then with it.</p>
<ReplicationLoss />
<p>Losing a cache fill usually creates a miss. Losing an invalidation can create a stale hit. That difference is why “it is only a cache” does not finish the recovery discussion. We must decide both how the service becomes available again and which answers it may return afterwards.</p>

<h2>Asynchronous replication and replication lag</h2>
<p>A Redis <strong>primary</strong> accepts writes. A <strong>replica</strong> follows a stream of changes from it. Replication is asynchronous by default: an ordinary successful write reply does not wait for every replica to apply that write. Redis documentation and command output also use the older term <em>master</em> for the primary.</p>
<p>Each copy can therefore be at a different point in the stream. The primary may have processed the deletion while a replica still contains the old key. Reading from that replica introduces another source of staleness, even before any failover. A healthy connection is not proof that a particular write has arrived.</p>
<p>Redis tracks progress using a replication ID and an <strong>offset</strong> in the replication stream. Offsets count stream bytes, not application updates or seconds of freshness. Comparing progress within the same replication history helps identify a gap; converting that gap into application impact requires knowing which work has not arrived.</p>
<p>The <strong>replication backlog</strong> retains recent stream data so a reconnecting replica may catch up through <strong>partial resynchronisation</strong>. If the required history is unavailable, it needs a full resynchronisation: an initial dataset plus subsequent changes. A reconnect can consequently be a small catch-up or a substantial recovery operation.</p>
<p>As a sizing illustration, a backlog retaining 64 MiB under a steady 8 MiB/s replication stream covers roughly eight seconds of stream history. It is not an eight-second guarantee: bursts, history changes and other resynchronisation conditions matter. Measure stream rate and disconnection behaviour. A full synchronisation also consumes network, memory and execution capacity just when the system may be recovering from a fault.</p>
<p>The native lab deliberately disconnects a replica after it has received the cached value 12. It then deletes the primary’s copy, kills that primary and manually promotes the disconnected replica. The new primary returns 12. This controlled experiment demonstrates what the surviving dataset contains; it does not claim that Sentinel always selects such a replica.</p>

<h2>WAIT and acknowledged-write loss</h2>
<p><code>WAIT 1 200</code> asks the current connection to wait up to 200 milliseconds for at least one replica to acknowledge its preceding writes. The result is a count, including when the timeout expires. Check that count. Sending WAIT on a different pooled connection does not establish the intended relationship to the earlier write.</p>
<p>In the disconnected-replica exercise, DEL returns 1, WAIT returns 0, and a read from the original primary still reports the key absent. The WAIT timeout did not roll back the deletion. It told the client that the requested acknowledgement level had not been established.</p>
<p>WAIT improves the evidence that other copies received a write. It does not make Redis strongly consistent, guarantee which copy a later failover will choose, or establish that those copies have durably stored the operation. Its timeout must also fit the application’s request budget; adding an unbounded wait would undermine the failure handling developed earlier.</p>
<p>The server-side settings <code>min-replicas-to-write</code> and <code>min-replicas-max-lag</code> can reject writes when too few sufficiently responsive replicas are available. They are a health-based admission rule, not a per-write acknowledgement. Their practical trade-off is reduced exposure to isolated writes at the cost of refusing more writes during faults.</p>
<p>For a cache, decide what insufficient replication evidence means. It may justify recording an invalidation for later repair, temporarily bypassing a suspect entry, or accepting bounded staleness. Blindly retrying does not invent a stronger guarantee. The original operation may already have happened, and the repair action must respect the <a href="/learn/databases/caching/clients-and-latency/">client’s deadlines and retry semantics</a>.</p>

<h2>RDB snapshots and AOF persistence</h2>
<p>Replication places another copy on another process. <strong>Persistence</strong> records state on storage so a process can reconstruct it after restarting. These solve different failure cases. Two in-memory copies can both disappear; one durable copy can remain unavailable while its machine is down.</p>
<p>An <strong>RDB snapshot</strong> records a dataset at a point in time. An <strong>append-only file</strong>, or <strong>AOF</strong>, records changes from which Redis can rebuild state. For an AOF, a write reaching an operating-system buffer and a completed <code>fsync</code> are separate events. Move the failure point below across the deletion and the next fsync.</p>
<PersistenceRecovery />
<p>At point 2, the in-memory key is empty. The snapshot still contains 12. In the periodic-fsync model, the durable AOF prefix also still ends at 12. At point 3, the completed fsync brings the deletion into that prefix, so replay now produces an empty key. Persistence must preserve invalidations as well as fills to reproduce the intended cache state.</p>
<p>Redis’s <code>appendfsync</code> policies choose when to request that storage boundary. <code>always</code> completes a fsync before replying to the corresponding writes, with batching possible. <code>everysec</code> normally syncs periodically, allowing recent writes to be lost. <code>no</code> leaves flushing to the operating system. The actual failure model and storage behaviour still matter; a policy name is not a guarantee against loss of the storage itself.</p>
<p>The usual “about a second” description of everysec is an operating expectation, not a universal promise under stalled storage or changed configuration. For example, <code>no-appendfsync-on-rewrite yes</code> allows fsync to be skipped during background persistence work. Inspect effective settings and persistence errors rather than inferring a recovery bound from one option.</p>
<p><code>WAITAOF</code>, available since Redis 7.2, can wait for preceding writes on the same connection to be acknowledged as fsynced locally and/or on replicas. It returns local and replica counts; again, inspect them. It strengthens the particular durability evidence requested, while retaining important limits around failover and surviving copies.</p>
<p>The lab makes two distinct observations. With snapshots, it saves 12, writes 15, kills Redis and recovers 12. With AOF and <code>appendfsync always</code>, it writes 12 then 15, receives <code>[1, 0]</code> from <code>WAITAOF 1 0 1000</code>, kills Redis and recovers 15. These are process-crash tests on a functioning host. They do not test sudden power loss, a lying storage device or destruction of the disk.</p>
<p>Persistence has operating costs as well as recovery benefits. Snapshot creation and AOF rewriting need resource headroom, as shown in <a href="/learn/databases/caching/memory-and-performance/">Redis memory and performance</a>. Redis 8.2 uses a multipart AOF with a manifest, base and incremental files; recovery procedures must treat them as a set. Test restoring the actual configured format rather than assuming one file copied at an arbitrary instant is sufficient.</p>

<h2>Redis Sentinel, quorum and failover</h2>
<p>A replica does not by itself decide when it should replace an unreachable primary. <strong>Redis Sentinel</strong> supplies monitoring, failover coordination and discovery for a non-clustered Redis deployment. It can promote a replica and tell clients where the new primary is.</p>
<p>The configured <strong>quorum</strong> determines how many Sentinels must agree that the primary has failed. Authorising a failover also needs a majority of the known Sentinels; a quorum set above that majority raises the requirement further. With five Sentinels and quorum two, two reports can establish failure while two votes are still insufficient to authorise promotion.</p>
<SentinelQuorum />
<p>Move the connected group from two to three. Now a majority can authorise the attempt. Remove the eligible replica: the votes remain, but there is nothing suitable to promote. Failure detection, authority to act and a usable surviving copy are separate requirements.</p>
<p>Place Sentinels across independent failure domains. Three processes on one machine cannot protect against losing that machine. In the native lab, three local Sentinels discover one another, observe a primary process being killed, promote its replica and converge on the new address. That verifies the mechanics locally; it does not exercise a zone failure or a network partition.</p>
<p>Clients need Sentinel-aware discovery and reconnection, using the monitored service name and reachable Sentinel addresses. Discovery yields an endpoint whose role must be checked. A hard-coded connection to yesterday’s primary does not follow a promotion automatically. Our lab explicitly reconnects its observer after promotion and checks ROLE; it is not a demonstration of a production client’s automatic recovery policy.</p>
<p>A partition makes the distinction particularly important. An old primary may remain reachable to some clients while the rest of the deployment promotes another copy. Successful replies on the old side can later be lost. Sentinel coordinates the topology; it does not turn asynchronous replication into an atomic transaction with your application database.</p>
<p>Also rehearse restart behaviour when persistence is disabled. An old primary that automatically restarts empty can propagate that empty dataset to its replicas if they follow it. “There was a warm replica” is not enough unless the recovery procedure preserves and selects the intended copy.</p>

<h2>Cache freshness after recovery</h2>
<p>A durable stale value is still stale. The source database may have changed while Redis was unavailable, or the recovered Redis history may precede an invalidation that the application already considers delivered.</p>
<p>Redis expiry times are stored as absolute timestamps. Downtime does not pause them, and restoring a snapshot does not restart the original TTL. In the lab, a short-lived key is saved, the server stops, and its expiry passes before restart; the recovered key is absent.</p>
<p>But a key can remain within its TTL and still be too old for the application. In the next model, the saved key expires at 90 seconds. The application’s optional age contract allows an observation less than 60 seconds old. At a restore time of 70 seconds, compare those two decisions.</p>
<RestoreFreshness />
<p>The age check must use the original source-observation timestamp, carried in the cached value. Replacing it with the restart time would turn old data into apparently young data. Even a valid age bound permits changes made since that observation; it does not prove equality with the latest source. Clock assumptions are part of this contract.</p>
<p>For entries requiring stronger guarantees, recovery may need authoritative revalidation or a new cache generation before reads resume. A generation marker stored only in the same Redis history can roll back with the entries it was meant to invalidate. The authority for the generation must survive the failure being defended against. The version protocols in <a href="/learn/databases/caching/invalidation/">cache invalidation and consistency</a> need this recovery boundary too.</p>
<p>A durable invalidation log helps repair missed work, but its consumer position matters. If the consumer has already marked a deletion delivered and Redis later loses that deletion, simply resuming at the next event will not repair it. Replaying a retained interval, reconciling source versions or changing generation are possible recovery designs. Each needs an explicit rule for when recovered entries become trustworthy.</p>

<h2>Design and test the recovery path</h2>
<p>Write down the failure you intend to survive: a process exit, a machine loss, a partition or loss of all cache copies. Then specify the permitted interruption, the allowed stale-answer window, and how much source traffic recovery may generate. <strong>Recovery time objective</strong> (RTO) sets the maximum acceptable recovery time; <strong>recovery point objective</strong> (RPO) sets the maximum acceptable age of the recovery point, expressing tolerated data loss as a time window. For a cache, neither replaces the freshness contract.</p>
<p>Choose persistence according to the cost of rebuilding and the consequences of restoring old entries. A disposable cache can reasonably start empty if the application can absorb a controlled warm-up. A persistent cache can reduce that work if recovered entries can be validated. Neither choice removes the need for the <a href="/learn/databases/caching/failure-handling/">bounded source-loading and recovery controls</a> developed earlier.</p>
<p>Measure recovery until useful application responses have resumed, not merely until a process reports ready. Include discovery, connection replacement, dataset loading, invalidation repair and hit-rate recovery. Observe source concurrency and tail latency throughout. Warming every key at once can turn a successful Redis restart into a source outage.</p>
<p>The <a href="/learn/databases/caching/examples/redis-recovery-lab.zip" download>downloadable recovery lab</a> runs the four experiments described here with Redis 8.2.2, Node 24+ and node-redis 6.3.0. It creates its own local processes and temporary data, asserts the results, then removes them. Its intentionally short Sentinel timers are lab settings. Extend failure testing in an isolated environment that matches your deployment’s actual storage, network, client and failure domains.</p>
<p>We now have a way to ask which copy contains which history, who may promote it, and whether the application should accept its answers. Next, <a href="/learn/databases/caching/#collection">Redis Cluster and hot keys</a> adds a different concern: dividing the keyspace across multiple primaries without assuming that every workload divides evenly.</p>

